      <!-- Detailed Review: Allo -->
      <div id="allo" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:16px;padding:32px;margin-bottom:64px;">
        <h3 style="color:white;font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:16px;">Allo, by The Mobile-First Company</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Start with the thing Allo does better than almost anyone in this category: it tells you the price, and the price is low. A Business seat is $32 a month billed yearly, or $45 month to month, and the AI Receptionist is one more seat at the same rate, so a solo agent pays the equivalent of about $64 a month annually or about $90 monthly. There is no per minute fee, so a busy Monday costs the same as a quiet Sunday, and that single fact puts Allo ahead of Smith.ai, Goodcall, RingCentral, Nextiva and CloudTalk, all of which either meter calls or charge a standalone per-agent rate. Around the answering you also get a real phone system, which is more than some of the vendors on this site offer: numbers in 80 or more countries, free number porting, SMS send and receive, call recording and transcription, an IVR, a shared inbox, a mobile app that is actually the product rather than a cut-down copy, and live chat and priority support. Add $50 a month for the Power Dialer and your team gets a parallel dialer for the whole office, and $50 a month more buys a HIPAA business associate agreement if you handle anything sensitive. There is no setup fee, no contract, changes to seats are prorated, and every plan starts with a 3 day free trial with the AI switched on. For a solo agent who loses deals to missed calls, this is the cheapest credible fix on the market and we are not going to argue otherwise.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          The provider is also refreshingly honest about a limitation that most competitors hide. Allo states that today its AI receptionist answers calls and does not place them, so outbound calling stays with your team. That is the right thing to say out loud, and it is the same shape as our own product: we answer, we follow up, we do not cold call for you. Its real estate receptionist is set up the way ours is, too, answering sign and listing calls from the listing details you supply, asking the buyer questions you would ask (budget, timeline, pre-approval, pets), booking the showing into Google Calendar, Outlook, Cal.com or Calendly, and handing off to the listing agent, with a standing rule never to negotiate price or comment on schools and safety. Calls are logged to your CRM, which Allo reaches through HubSpot, Pipedrive, Attio, Salesforce or Zapier, so it works with the database you already pay for rather than asking you to change it.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Now the limits, and the honest ones are four. First, and this is the one that matters most in real estate, Allo does not hold a WhatsApp conversation. It answers calls, it sends an SMS text back when you miss a call, and it has a shared SMS inbox, but a buyer who opens WhatsApp on a Sunday still does not get an AI reply. Plenty of markets run most of their first contact through WhatsApp, which is exactly why we sell the WhatsApp receptionist as its own $297 product. Second, the AI is English first. Allo advertises a French voice among its options but makes no Spanish claim, so a Spanish speaking buyer calling after hours gets an English greeting unless you have a person on the line, and Spanish is the language of a large share of the buyers a United States agent meets. Third, it is a phone line you rent by the seat. Every agent needs a seat, the receptionist adds one more, and the meter never stops: $64 a month for one person becomes about $2,304 a year for a five person team with the receptionist, and it renews. Fourth, it is narrower than the word "AI" suggests. It answers and it logs. It does not build your website, it does not supply a single lead, and it does not run your follow-up as a campaign.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          One more honest note, on reviews, because Allo's review base is real and worth reading rather than hiding. G2 shows roughly 4.6 to 4.7 out of 5 across about 200 reviews, and G2's own summary of the negatives lists a lack of picture messaging, SMS features reviewers miss, intermittent call quality and app lag, some difficulty with call forwarding, and a desktop app several users call weaker than the mobile one. Two quotes capture it: one reviewer wrote that it was a shame SMS messages are no longer unlimited, and another described inconsistent call quality and said they had reached out to the team with no update for over a month. Trustpilot is thinner at 4.2 out of 5 across 24 reviews, warm on call clarity and support, with a handful of 1 star reviews about being charged after cancelling and support that went quiet. An independent test of 47 calls over three weeks reported latency under 200ms with no dropped calls and the receptionist routing correctly around 87 percent of the time. None of that is damning, and most of it is better than the average vendor on this site, but it is the reason we tell every reader the same thing: use the free trial before you trust a page like this one, ours included.
        </p>
